<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">French Community</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1958">1958</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The French Community was an association created by de Gaulle's constitution of 1958 to replace the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-union">French Union</a>, offering colonies autonomy within a French framework. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> immediately chose independence instead, and within a few years most African members became independent, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sub-saharan-africa">sub-Saharan Africa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/french-community.jpg" alt="French Community" width="320" height="332" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thommy · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Standard_of_the_French_Community.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-union">French Union</a> of 1946 had held that there were no French colonies, only metropolitan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the overseas <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> and the overseas territories forming one France, but in practice power stayed centralised in the French Parliament. An enabling law of 31 January 1956 abandoned assimilation in favour of autonomy, to quiet concern over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>.</p><p>The one million French settlers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> demonstrated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> on 13 May 1958, the crisis ended the Fourth Republic, and General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a> was recalled to write a new constitution. It gave overseas territories the right to request independence. A referendum on 28 September 1958 approved it everywhere except French Guinea, which chose complete independence and became independent on 2 October 1958.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The territorial assemblies had until 4 February 1959 to choose between keeping overseas territory status, becoming a state of the Community or becoming an overseas <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>. Twelve African territories chose to be states of the Community: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a>, French Sudan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-coast">Ivory Coast</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a>, Middle Congo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>, Ubangi-Shari and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-volta">Upper Volta</a>.</p><p>The Community did not fully function until 1959, and its executive council held six sessions that year. Foreign affairs, defence, the currency, economic policy and control of raw materials were common matters, so that, under an appearance of equality, the states' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> was restricted and the precedence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> reaffirmed. The Senate of the Community had little power and was abolished in March 1961.</p><p>The constitutional act of 4 June 1960 allowed states to become independent and still remain members. In 1960 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-federation">Mali Federation</a> and the Malagasy Republic became independent within the Community in June, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-volta">Upper Volta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-coast">Ivory Coast</a> became independent and left it in August. Chad, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-african-republic">Central African Republic</a>, Congo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabon">Gabon</a> became independent within it, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a> left it in November. By 1961 only six states still belonged, and the Community had ceased to function in practice. Its provisions stayed in the constitution until Constitutional Act 95-880 of 4 August 1995 repealed them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The President of the French Republic was President of the Community and was represented in each member state by a High Commissioner. The Community also had an Executive Council of the heads of government and ministers for common affairs, and a Court of Arbitration of seven judges nominated by the President. The French government remained responsible for security. Africans in the Community did not form a majority voting bloc, since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> did not wish to become a colony of its colonies.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “French Community” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>De Gaulle's <b>French Community</b> of 1958 offered autonomy within a French framework.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In the referendum of 28 September 1958 on Charles de Gaulle’s <b>French Community</b>, Guinea under Ahmed Sékou Touré voted no and became independent on 2 October 1958; the other territories voted yes and became autonomous republics within the Community.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-west-africa">French West Africa · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>French Upper Volta became a self-governing colony within the <b>French Community</b> on 11 December 1958, and attained full independence as the Republic of Upper Volta on 5 August 1960.</blockquote><a href="/state/burkina-faso">Burkina Faso</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1958 the four territories became autonomous republics within the <b>French Community</b> and the federation was dissolved, its members becoming independent in August 1960.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-equatorial-africa">French Equatorial Africa</a></li><li><blockquote>Houphouet-Boigny, who also served as a minister in Paris, led the territory to autonomy within the <b>French Community</b> in 1958 and to independence on 7 August 1960.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-ivory-coast">French Ivory Coast</a></li><li><blockquote>Senegal became an autonomous republic within the <b>French Community</b> in 1958, joined the Mali Federation in 1959, and became fully independent on 20 August 1960 under Leopold Sedar Senghor.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-senegal">French Senegal</a></li></ul></section>
